WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.070 --> 00:00:06.290
In 1928, Fleming saw a fungus called
Penicillium stop bacteria growing.

2
00:00:06.340 --> 00:00:10.260
By the 1940s, penicillin
was in mass production.

3
00:00:10.310 --> 00:00:13.260
Once-deadly infections could be cured.

4
00:00:13.310 --> 00:00:16.250
Today, some infections no longer respond,

5
00:00:16.300 --> 00:00:18.500
and a few are untreatable.

6
00:00:18.550 --> 00:00:23.050
How can a drug that once cured an infection
stop working against the very same

7
00:00:23.100 --> 00:00:24.220
germ?

8
00:00:24.270 --> 00:00:28.760
In this mission, you'll follow a population
of bacteria as a dose of antibiotic

9
00:00:28.810 --> 00:00:29.880
arrives.

10
00:00:29.930 --> 00:00:32.830
Then you'll see the same
pattern outside medicine,

11
00:00:32.880 --> 00:00:35.350
in a field of weeds
sprayed with herbicide,

12
00:00:35.400 --> 00:00:37.170
year after year.

13
00:00:37.220 --> 00:00:42.170
Your mission: explain how a drug that
once cured an infection can stop working

14
00:00:42.220 --> 00:00:43.810
against the same germ.
